{
 "id": "exp-03-01",
 "slug": "solving-by-matching-bases",
 "title": "Solving by Matching Bases",
 "courseId": "exponential-functions",
 "chapterId": "ch3-exponential-equations",
 "minutes": 10,
 "steps": [
  {
   "id": "c1",
   "kind": "concept",
   "figure": "exp-curve-growth",
   "body": "To solve bˣ = k, rewrite k as a power of the **same base** b. Then the exponents must be equal. For 2ˣ = 8: since 8 = 2³, we have 2ˣ = 2³, so x = 3."
  },
  {
   "id": "i1",
   "kind": "interactive",
   "body": "Match the base.",
   "widget": {
    "type": "numeric",
    "prompt": "Solve 2^x = 8.",
    "answer": 3,
    "tolerance": 0,
    "commonErrors": [
     {
      "value": 4,
      "feedback": "This isn't 2·x = 8. Ask how many 2's multiply to 8: 2·2·2 = 8, so x = 3."
     },
     {
      "value": 64,
      "feedback": "That's 2⁶. You want 2ˣ = 8 = 2³, so x = 3."
     }
    ],
    "fallbackFeedback": "8 = 2³, so x = 3."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "k1",
   "kind": "check",
   "body": "A base of 3.",
   "conceptTag": "exp-match-base",
   "explanationVariants": [
    "81 = 3⁴ (3·3·3·3), so x = 4.",
    "Count the 3's: 3 → 9 → 27 → 81 is four steps, so x = 4."
   ],
   "widget": {
    "type": "numeric",
    "prompt": "Solve 3^x = 81.",
    "answer": 4,
    "tolerance": 0,
    "commonErrors": [
     {
      "value": 27,
      "feedback": "27 = 3³, one power short. 81 = 3⁴, so x = 4."
     },
     {
      "value": 3,
      "feedback": "3 is the base, not the exponent. 81 = 3⁴, so x = 4."
     }
    ],
    "fallbackFeedback": "81 = 3⁴, so x = 4."
   },
   "variant": {
    "gen": "a1-exponential",
    "form": "exp-match-base__numeric"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c2",
   "kind": "concept",
   "body": "The base can be anything. For 5ˣ = 25: since 25 = 5², x = 2. A good question to ask: “how many times do I multiply b by itself to reach k?” That count is the exponent.",
   "figure": "exp-match-25"
  },
  {
   "id": "i2",
   "kind": "interactive",
   "body": "Count the fives.",
   "widget": {
    "type": "numeric",
    "prompt": "Solve 5^x = 25.",
    "answer": 2,
    "tolerance": 0,
    "commonErrors": [
     {
      "value": 5,
      "feedback": "This isn't 5·x = 25. 25 = 5², so x = 2."
     },
     {
      "value": 3,
      "feedback": "5³ = 125, too big. 25 = 5², so x = 2."
     }
    ],
    "fallbackFeedback": "25 = 5², so x = 2."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c3",
   "kind": "concept",
   "body": "Sometimes the target hides a clean power: 4ˣ = 64 → 64 = 4³, so x = 3. Keep rewriting the target as a power of the base until the exponent is obvious.",
   "figure": "exp-match-64"
  },
  {
   "id": "i3",
   "kind": "interactive",
   "body": "Powers of ten.",
   "widget": {
    "type": "numeric",
    "prompt": "Solve 10^x = 1000.",
    "answer": 3,
    "tolerance": 0,
    "commonErrors": [
     {
      "value": 100,
      "feedback": "100 = 10², not the answer. 1000 = 10³, so x = 3."
     },
     {
      "value": 2,
      "feedback": "10² = 100. To reach 1000 you need 10³, so x = 3."
     }
    ],
    "fallbackFeedback": "1000 = 10³, so x = 3."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "k2",
   "kind": "check",
   "body": "Higher power of two.",
   "conceptTag": "exp-match-base",
   "explanationVariants": [
    "32 = 2⁵ (2·2·2·2·2), so x = 5.",
    "Double from 1: 2, 4, 8, 16, 32 is five steps, so x = 5."
   ],
   "widget": {
    "type": "numeric",
    "prompt": "Solve 2^x = 32.",
    "answer": 5,
    "tolerance": 0,
    "commonErrors": [
     {
      "value": 16,
      "feedback": "16 = 2⁴, one short. 32 = 2⁵, so x = 5."
     },
     {
      "value": 30,
      "feedback": "This isn't 2·x = 32. 32 = 2⁵, so x = 5."
     }
    ],
    "fallbackFeedback": "32 = 2⁵, so x = 5."
   },
   "variant": {
    "gen": "a1-exponential",
    "form": "exp-match-base__numeric"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "k3",
   "kind": "check",
   "body": "When the exponent isn't whole.",
   "conceptTag": "exp-match-base",
   "explanationVariants": [
    "Rewrite both sides base 2: 4ˣ = 2^(2x) and 8 = 2³, so 2x = 3 and x = 3/2.",
    "4^(3/2) = (√4)³ = 2³ = 8, so x = 3/2."
   ],
   "widget": {
